==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|aqueste}} (later conflating with {{m|en|enqueste}}), from {{der|en|fro|aquest}}, ({{cog|fr|acquêt}}), from {{der|en|VL.|*acquaerere|*acquaesitum}}, ultimately from {{der|en|la|quaero|quaerere|t=seek}}. {{doublet|en|acquis}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkwɛst/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-acquest.wav|Audio (RP)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|rare}} [[acquisition|Acquisition]]; the thing gained.
#* {{RQ:Bacon War with Spain|passage=the Palatinate , it is a maxim in state , that all countries of new '''acquest''', till they be settled , are rather matters of burden than of strength}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} [[property|Property]] acquired by purchase, gift, or otherwise than by inheritance.

====Usage notes====
Bouvier, 1856, [http://www.constitution.org/bouv/bouvier_a.htm] from which the Webster derives, uses the spelling ''[[acquets]]''.

